// longQuestions3.en.js
// HKDSE Biology — Long questions part 3 (Topics 26–37) — English (UK) version
// Loaded when the app language is English (UK). Structure matches longQuestions3.js (zh-HK).
// Each chapter has 15 long questions with model answers (question and answer only, no marking).

const LQ3_EN = [
  {
    topicNo: 26,
    name: "Basic Genetics",
    questions: [
      { q: "Explain why the sex of the offspring is not mainly determined by the mother.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "The mother produces only one type of egg, which carries one X chromosome.\nThe father produces two types of sperm, one carrying an X chromosome and one carrying a Y chromosome.\nThe sex of the offspring depends on which type of sperm fertilises the egg.\nTherefore the sex is not determined by the mother." },
      { q: "Maggie's father is red-green colour blind, while Maggie has normal colour vision. Without using a genetic diagram, deduce Maggie's genotype.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Since Maggie's father is red-green colour blind, his X chromosome should carry the recessive allele for colour blindness.\nMaggie is female and should have inherited the X chromosome carrying that recessive allele from her father.\nOn the other hand, Maggie has normal colour vision, so she should have an X chromosome carrying the dominant normal-vision allele.\nTherefore Maggie is heterozygous (a carrier)." },
      { q: "In guinea pigs, black fur (B) is dominant over white fur (b). Two heterozygous black guinea pigs are crossed.\n(a) Write down the genotypes of the parents.\n(b) Using a genetic diagram, show the genotype and phenotype ratios of the offspring.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "(a) Parental genotypes: Bb × Bb.\n(b) Genetic diagram:\nGametes: B, b (female) × B, b (male).\nOffspring genotypes: BB : Bb : bb = 1 : 2 : 1.\nOffspring phenotypes: black (BB, Bb) : white (bb) = 3 : 1.\n(A Punnett square may be drawn.)" },
      { q: "Explain Mendel's law of segregation and law of independent assortment.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Law of segregation: when gametes are formed, the pair of alleles separates and each gamete contains only one of them.\nLaw of independent assortment: when gametes are formed, the segregation of the alleles of each gene is independent of the segregation of the alleles of other genes (genes on different chromosomes assort freely).\nThe two laws explain the ratios of monohybrid (3:1) and dihybrid (9:3:3:1) crosses." },
      { q: "Write down the alleles of the ABO blood group system, and explain the relationship between IA and IB.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The ABO blood group is controlled by multiple alleles: IA, IB and i.\nIA and IB are codominant (both are expressed, producing group AB).\nIA and IB are completely dominant over i (IAi is group A, IBi is group B).\nii is group O; group O is the 'universal donor' because its red blood cells have no antigens A or B." },
      { q: "Explain why more males than females are affected by X-linked recessive diseases (such as red-green colour blindness and haemophilia).", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Males have only one X chromosome, so one recessive allele is enough for the disease to be expressed (hemizygous).\nFemales have two X chromosomes and need two recessive alleles (homozygous) to be affected; heterozygous females are carriers and are not affected.\nTherefore males are more likely to be affected.\nA father does not pass the X-linked gene to his son (the son gets a Y chromosome from the father)." },
      { q: "Write down the definition of a test cross, and explain how to determine the genotype of an individual showing a dominant phenotype.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "A test cross is crossing the unknown dominant individual with a homozygous recessive individual.\nIf all the offspring show the dominant phenotype, the unknown individual is homozygous dominant (e.g. BB × bb → all Bb).\nIf the offspring show a 1:1 dominant:recessive ratio, the unknown individual is heterozygous (e.g. Bb × bb → Bb : bb = 1:1).\nThe phenotype ratio of the offspring reveals the genotype of the parent." },
      { q: "Explain why DNA is suitable as the genetic material (four characteristics).", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The base sequence forms the genetic code and can carry a large amount of information.\nThe molecule is stable and can preserve genetic information.\nIt can replicate accurately (complementary pairing), ensuring that genetic information is passed to offspring.\nIt can mutate, providing the raw material for genetic variation.\n(Or: double-stranded structure, base pairing A-T, C-G.)" },
      { q: "Explain the factors causing genetic variation.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Independent assortment of homologous chromosomes during meiosis (producing gametes with different gene combinations).\nCrossing over (non-sister chromatids exchange segments).\nRandom fertilisation (male and female gametes with different gene combinations fuse randomly).\nMutation (gene mutation produces new alleles).\nEnvironmental factors affect the expression of some genes." },
      { q: "A colour-blind man marries a carrier woman with normal vision.\n(a) Write down the parental genotypes.\n(b) Using a genetic diagram, find the probability of their children being colour blind.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Let Xᵇ = colour-blind allele, XB = normal allele.\n(a) Father: XᵇY; mother: XBXᵇ.\n(b) Genetic diagram:\nGametes: father Xᵇ, Y; mother XB, Xᵇ.\nOffspring: XBXᵇ (carrier daughter), XᵇXᵇ (colour-blind daughter), XBY (normal son), XᵇY (colour-blind son).\nProbability: 1/2 of all children are colour blind; 1/4 are colour-blind sons; 1/4 are colour-blind daughters." },
      { q: "Explain why blood group is discontinuous variation, while height is continuous variation.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Blood group is controlled by multiple alleles at a single gene locus and appears as a few distinct categories (A, B, AB, O), so it is discontinuous variation.\nHeight is controlled by many genes and is also affected by the environment, so individuals show a continuous distribution, making it continuous variation.\nDiscontinuous variation has distinct, separable categories; continuous variation has no clear category boundaries." },
      { q: "Explain the probability of a child being affected when both parents are heterozygous carriers of an autosomal recessive disease.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Parental genotypes: Aa × Aa.\nOffspring genotypes: AA : Aa : aa = 1 : 2 : 1.\nProbability of the child being affected (aa): 1/4.\nProbability of the child being a carrier (Aa): 1/2.\nProbability of the child not being affected (AA or Aa): 3/4." },
      { q: "Write down the difference between genotype and phenotype, and explain how the environment affects the phenotype.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Genotype is the combination of alleles of an individual (its genetic make-up); phenotype is the observable characteristics of an individual.\nPhenotype = genotype + environmental effects.\nFor example, plants with the same genotype may differ in height and colour when grown in different light/dark conditions.\nEnvironmental factors (temperature, nutrition, light) can affect the expression of genes." },
      { q: "Explain why a mutation is inherited by the offspring only when it occurs in gametes or the cells that produce gametes.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Only mutations in gametes (sperm, egg) or the germ cells that produce gametes will enter fertilisation and be passed to the next generation.\nMutations in body cells only affect the individual itself, do not enter the gametes and cannot be inherited.\nTherefore inherited mutations must occur in the germ cells." },
      { q: "A couple both have normal vision, but their first child is a colour-blind boy.\n(a) Deduce the genotypes of the parents.\n(b) Explain the probability of their next child being a colour-blind boy.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "(a) Father: XBY (normal); mother: XBXᵇ (carrier, because she gave birth to a colour-blind son XᵇY, so she must carry Xᵇ).\n(b) Genetic diagram: gametes father XB, Y; mother XB, Xᵇ.\nOffspring: XBXB, XBXᵇ, XBY, XᵇY.\nProbability of the next child being a colour-blind boy (XᵇY): 1/4." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 27,
    name: "Molecular Genetics",
    questions: [
      { q: "Describe the process from the DNA base sequence to protein synthesis (transcription and translation).", marks: "6 marks", answer: "Transcription takes place in the nucleus: the DNA double helix unwinds, and one strand becomes the template for synthesising mRNA.\nUnder the catalysis of RNA polymerase, free RNA nucleotides join in complementary sequence along the template, forming mRNA.\nThe mRNA leaves the nucleus, enters the cytoplasm and attaches to a ribosome.\nTranslation takes place on the ribosome: tRNA molecules carry specific amino acids.\nThe anticodons of tRNA pair complementarily with the codons of mRNA.\nPeptide bonds form between adjacent amino acids to form a polypeptide; at a stop codon the polypeptide is released and folds into a protein." },
      { q: "Explain why a gene mutation in mitochondrial DNA can affect oxidative phosphorylation.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "A gene mutation involves a change in the nucleotide/base sequence of a single gene.\nThe polypeptide produced by the mutated gene has a different amino acid sequence.\nThe polypeptide folds into an enzyme with a different shape / three-dimensional conformation / active site.\nThis enzyme loses its function in oxidative phosphorylation and cannot produce a functional enzyme." },
      { q: "Write down the process of DNA replication (semiconservative replication).", marks: "5 marks", answer: "The hydrogen bonds of the DNA double helix break and the two strands unwind.\nEach strand acts as a template.\nFree nucleotides join by complementary base pairing (A-T, C-G).\nDNA polymerase catalyses the formation of the new strand.\nThe result is two new DNA molecules, each containing one old strand and one new strand (semiconservative replication)." },
      { q: "Explain the three characteristics of the genetic code (triplet, degeneracy, universality).", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Triplet: every three bases (a codon) encode one amino acid.\nDegeneracy: several codons can encode the same amino acid (e.g. leucine has multiple codons), reducing the effect of mutations.\nUniversality: almost all organisms share the same genetic code.\nThe codons do not overlap and are read continuously." },
      { q: "Compare DNA replication, transcription and translation in terms of products and location.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "DNA replication: takes place in the nucleus, using DNA as the template to synthesise new DNA, for cell division.\nTranscription: takes place in the nucleus, using one strand of DNA as the template to synthesise mRNA.\nTranslation: takes place on the ribosomes in the cytoplasm, using mRNA as the template, with tRNA carrying amino acids to synthesise a polypeptide (protein).\nThe three processes together transmit and express genetic information." },
      { q: "Explain the different effects of a 'substitution' mutation and an 'insertion/deletion' mutation on proteins.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Substitution: changes only one codon, and the effect may be small or none (because of degeneracy), changing one amino acid or none (silent mutation).\nInsertion/deletion: causes a frameshift mutation, shifting the reading frame, so all the following codons change and the amino acid sequence of the protein is completely altered, usually making the protein non-functional.\nTherefore insertion/deletion usually has a greater and more severe effect." },
      { q: "Write down the enzymes or structures involved in transcription and translation (RNA polymerase, tRNA, ribosomes, etc.).", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Transcription: catalysed by RNA polymerase, synthesising mRNA from the DNA template in the nucleus.\nTranslation: mRNA, ribosomes (rRNA + proteins) and tRNA (carrying amino acids, with anticodons pairing with codons) are involved.\nPeptide bonds join amino acids to form a polypeptide.\n(DNA polymerase is used in DNA replication and is not involved in transcription/translation.)" },
      { q: "Explain the concept of 'one gene – one polypeptide'.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "The base sequence of one gene determines the amino acid sequence of one polypeptide.\nA protein may consist of one or more polypeptides.\nA gene determines the traits of an organism by controlling the synthesis of polypeptides." },
      { q: "Write down the comparison between DNA and RNA in structure and function.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Structure: DNA is a double-stranded double helix, containing deoxyribose and thymine (T); RNA is single-stranded, containing ribose and uracil (U).\nBases: DNA contains A, T, G, C; RNA contains A, U, G, C.\nFunction: DNA stores genetic information; RNA participates in protein synthesis (mRNA, tRNA, rRNA).\nDNA is in the nucleus; RNA is in the nucleus and the cytoplasm." },
      { q: "Explain why only one strand of DNA serves as the template during transcription.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "The two strands of DNA have complementary base sequences.\nOnly one strand (the template strand) is used by RNA polymerase as the template for synthesising mRNA.\nThe base sequence of mRNA is complementary to the template strand and identical to the other (coding) strand (except T is replaced by U).\nTherefore only one strand needs to serve as the template to synthesise the correct mRNA." },
      { q: "Write down the structure and function of tRNA, and explain the relationship between anticodons and codons.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "tRNA is a single-stranded RNA with a specific amino acid at one end and three bases (the anticodon) at the other.\nThe anticodon of tRNA pairs complementarily with the codon on mRNA.\nEach tRNA carries only one type of amino acid, ensuring that amino acids are arranged in the order of the mRNA codons.\nThrough the pairing of tRNA, the genetic code of mRNA is translated into the amino acid sequence of a protein." },
      { q: "A gene in a student has a 'substitution' mutation, changing CTT to CTA. If both CTT and CTA encode the same amino acid, predict whether the protein is affected, and explain.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "The protein is not affected.\nBecause CTT and CTA encode the same amino acid (the genetic code is degenerate).\nThe substitution does not change the amino acid sequence, so the protein function is unchanged (silent mutation)." },
      { q: "Explain why the effect of a gene mutation can be large or small, giving an example.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "The effect depends on the type of mutation: a substitution may change only one amino acid or none (degeneracy), so the effect can be small.\nInsertion/deletion causes a frameshift, with a large effect, usually making the protein non-functional.\nIf the mutation is in a non-coding region, it may not affect the protein.\nExample: sickle cell anaemia is caused by a single base T being substituted by A in the beta-globin gene (one amino acid change), affecting the function of haemoglobin." },
      { q: "Write down the types of chromosomal mutations (structural and numerical), giving one example of each.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Structural changes: deletion, duplication, inversion, translocation.\nNumerical changes: failure of homologous chromosomes or sister chromatids to separate during meiosis, forming aneuploids, e.g. Down syndrome (one extra chromosome 21, trisomy).\nChromosomal mutations affect many genes and usually have a large effect." },
      { q: "Explain why DNA replication is called 'semiconservative replication'.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "After replication, each new DNA molecule contains one old (parental) strand and one newly synthesised strand.\nThe old strand is conserved (semiconserved) as the template.\nThis ensures that genetic information is accurately passed to the daughter cells." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 28,
    name: "Biotechnology",
    questions: [
      { q: "Describe the main steps of producing human insulin using recombinant DNA technology.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "Obtain a DNA fragment carrying the human insulin gene from human cells.\nCut the target gene and the bacterial plasmid with the same restriction enzyme, producing the same sticky ends.\nInsert the target gene into the opened plasmid.\nJoin the target gene and the plasmid with DNA ligase, forming a recombinant plasmid.\nIntroduce the recombinant plasmid into bacteria (e.g. Escherichia coli).\nCulture the bacteria to produce large amounts of human insulin, then extract and purify it." },
      { q: "Explain how gel electrophoresis separates DNA fragments, and write down two applications of DNA fingerprinting.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "DNA fragments are placed at the cathode end of a gel plate.\nBecause DNA contains phosphate groups and is negatively charged, it moves towards the anode in an electric field.\nSmaller DNA fragments pass through the gel more easily and move faster than larger fragments.\nAfter some time, the DNA fragments are separated by molecular size and bands are visible after staining.\nApplications: providing evidence in forensic science, paternity testing, identifying disaster victims, deducing evolutionary relationships (any two)." },
      { q: "Write down the roles of restriction enzymes and DNA ligase in recombinant DNA technology.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Restriction enzymes: recognise specific base sequences and cut DNA ('molecular scissors'), producing sticky ends.\nDNA ligase: joins the sticky ends of the target gene and the plasmid ('molecular glue'), forming a recombinant plasmid.\nThe two enzymes work together to insert a foreign gene into a vector.\n(The same restriction enzyme cuts both the target gene and the vector to ensure complementary sticky ends.)" },
      { q: "Explain why plasmids are suitable as vectors in genetic engineering.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Plasmids are small circular DNA molecules in bacteria that can replicate independently.\nPlasmids can be cut by restriction enzymes and have a foreign gene inserted.\nRecombinant plasmids can be introduced into bacteria, where they replicate and express the foreign gene.\nPlasmids carry marker genes (such as antibiotic resistance genes) for selecting successfully transformed bacteria." },
      { q: "Explain the principle of PCR (polymerase chain reaction), and write down two applications.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "PCR is used to amplify a specific DNA fragment in large quantities.\nDenaturation (95°C): the double-stranded DNA unwinds.\nAnnealing (55°C): primers bind to the template.\nExtension (72°C): DNA polymerase synthesises the new strand.\nRepeating many cycles amplifies the DNA fragment exponentially.\nApplications: forensic analysis (PCR-STR), detection of pathogens, diagnosis of genetic diseases, identifying GM organisms (any two)." },
      { q: "Write down the applications of genetic engineering (medicine, agriculture, industry).", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Medicine: producing human insulin and growth hormone; gene therapy for genetic diseases; vaccines.\nAgriculture and food industry: improving crops (insect resistance, herbicide resistance), increasing yield, GM food.\nIndustry: genetically modified bacteria for efficient metal extraction; enhancing oil-degrading bacteria (bioremediation).\nThe applications are based on recombinant DNA technology to introduce target genes into organisms." },
      { q: "Explain how DNA fingerprinting is used in paternity testing.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "DNA fingerprinting uses the differences in the length of highly variable regions of DNA (non-coding repeated sequences).\nDNA is extracted from the child and the parents, cut with restriction enzymes, separated by gel electrophoresis and stained to show bands.\nHalf of the child's DNA bands should match the father and half should match the mother (the child gets half of its DNA from each parent).\nIf all the child's DNA bands can be found in the alleged father, paternity is supported; otherwise it is excluded.\n(Except for identical twins, each individual's DNA fingerprint is almost unique.)" },
      { q: "Explain why genetically modified bacteria (such as E. coli) are suitable for producing human proteins.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Bacteria reproduce quickly and can be cultured in large quantities, giving high production efficiency.\nThe cost of culture is low.\nRecombinant DNA technology can introduce human genes into bacteria, which can express human proteins.\nLarge amounts of pure human proteins (such as insulin) can be purified, which are purer and safer than those extracted from animals (fewer immune reactions)." },
      { q: "Write down the aims and benefits of the Human Genome Project.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Aims: to find out the base sequence of human DNA; to identify all genes and their positions (gene mapping); to store them in databases.\nBenefits: developing new diagnostic tests and treatments; assessing the risk of mutagens; promoting agricultural productivity; finding evolutionary relationships; promoting precision medicine.\nThe project produces a large amount of bioinformatics data." },
      { q: "Explain the benefits and potential risks of GM crops.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Benefits: higher yield, resistance to insects and herbicides, improved nutrition, tolerance to drought and salt, and reduced use of pesticides.\nRisks: genes may flow into wild populations (gene pollution); effects on non-target organisms (e.g. toxins affecting beneficial insects); reduction of biodiversity; possible allergic reactions (need assessment).\nRegulation and assessment are needed before planting." },
      { q: "Write down the DNA regions used in DNA fingerprinting, and explain why these regions are suitable for identifying individuals.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Highly variable regions of DNA are used (non-coding repeated sequences, such as short tandem repeats, STR).\nThe length of these regions varies greatly between individuals (highly polymorphic).\nExcept for identical twins, each person's DNA fingerprint is almost unique.\nTherefore they can be used to identify individuals, in paternity testing and forensic analysis." },
      { q: "Compare somatic cell gene therapy and germline gene therapy.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Somatic cell gene therapy: affects only the cells of the patient; the changes are not inherited by the offspring.\nGermline gene therapy: changes the genes of gametes or embryos; the changes can be inherited by the offspring.\nGermline gene therapy raises great ethical controversy and is prohibited in most countries.\nSomatic cell gene therapy is safer and more widely used (e.g. CAR-T, SCID)." },
      { q: "Explain why insulin produced by genetic engineering is better than insulin extracted from pigs.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The amino acid sequence of genetically engineered insulin is identical to human insulin.\nPig insulin differs from human insulin and may cause immune reactions.\nRecombinant insulin is highly pure, with few impurities, and can be produced in large quantities cheaply.\nTherefore it is safer and more suitable for long-term use by diabetic patients." },
      { q: "Write down the definition of a 'transgenic organism', giving two examples.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "A transgenic organism is an organism that contains genes from another species.\nExamples: E. coli carrying the human insulin gene; insect-resistant transgenic maize (Bt maize); transgenic soybean.\nTransgenic technology introduces a target gene from one organism into another." },
      { q: "Explain why DNA is negatively charged, and the role of this property in gel electrophoresis.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "DNA contains phosphate groups, and the phosphate groups carry a negative charge.\nIn an electric field, the negatively charged DNA fragments move towards the positive pole (anode).\nGel electrophoresis uses this property to separate DNA: smaller fragments move faster and larger fragments move slower, separating them by size." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 29,
    name: "Biodiversity",
    questions: [
      { q: "Compare plant X (a moss) and plant Y (a flowering plant) in terms of vascular tissue, and explain why plant Y can grow taller.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Plant X (moss) has no vascular bundles; plant Y has vascular bundles (containing xylem).\nThe vascular bundles contain xylem tissue / thick-walled cells, providing better support to the plant.\nTherefore plant Y can grow taller.\nThe xylem can also transport water to higher positions.\nMosses grow in damp environments because they have no cuticle to prevent water loss and no vascular bundles to transport water." },
      { q: "Explain why a dichotomous key cannot be used to deduce the evolutionary relationships between organisms.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "A dichotomous key identifies a group of organisms according to observable external morphological features.\nThese morphological features may not be related to evolutionary / phylogenetic relationships.\nTherefore evolutionary relationships cannot be deduced from a dichotomous key alone." },
      { q: "Write down the three levels of biodiversity, and explain the significance of each.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Species diversity: the number and abundance of different species in an ecosystem.\nGenetic diversity: the genetic differences between individuals of the same species; high diversity increases the ability to adapt to environmental changes.\nEcosystem diversity: the types of different habitats and communities.\nTogether these make up biodiversity, which is important for ecosystem stability and human resources." },
      { q: "Explain why high genetic diversity is beneficial to the survival of a species.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "High genetic diversity means large genetic differences between individuals.\nWhen the environment changes (e.g. climate, disease), some individuals may have gene combinations that are adaptive and survive.\nThe surviving individuals reproduce, passing the favourable genes to their offspring.\nA population with low genetic diversity has a weak ability to adapt to environmental changes and is more likely to become extinct." },
      { q: "Write down the reasons for conserving biodiversity (economic, ecological, aesthetic, moral).", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Economic: provides food, medicines, timber and other resources.\nEcological: maintains ecosystem stability and nutrient cycling (e.g. decomposers, pollinators).\nAesthetic: natural landscapes and organisms have ornamental value for people to enjoy.\nMoral: humans have a responsibility to protect other species and the natural environment.\nThe loss of biodiversity is irreversible and has far-reaching effects." },
      { q: "Compare in-situ conservation and ex-situ conservation, giving one example of each.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "In-situ conservation: conserving organisms in their natural habitats, e.g. setting up country parks, marine parks, nature reserves, Ramsar wetlands.\nEx-situ conservation: conserving organisms outside their natural habitats, e.g. breeding endangered species in zoos, storing seeds in seed banks, growing rare plants in botanical gardens.\nIn-situ conservation preserves complete ecosystems and is the most fundamental method; ex-situ conservation is used for species with very small numbers." },
      { q: "Explain the effects of invasive alien species on the local ecology.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Invasive alien species compete with local species for food, habitats and resources.\nThey may prey on local species, spread diseases and change habitats.\nLocal species lack defences against the invaders, so their numbers decline or they become extinct.\nThis reduces biodiversity and disrupts the ecological balance.\nExamples: golden apple snail, red fire ant, Mikania micrantha." },
      { q: "Write down the classification of the Three Domain Six Kingdom system.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Three domains: Bacteria, Archaea, Eukarya.\nSix kingdoms: Eubacteria, Archaebacteria, Protista, Fungi, Plantae, Animalia.\nEubacteria: genetic material is circular DNA free in the cytoplasm, cell wall made of peptidoglycan.\nArchaebacteria: cell wall composition differs from bacteria; they can survive in extreme environments." },
      { q: "Explain why the mangrove ecosystem is very important to Hong Kong.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Mangroves provide habitats and breeding grounds for many organisms (fish, shrimp, crabs, birds), maintaining biodiversity.\nThe roots of mangroves stabilise the sediment and reduce coastal erosion, protecting the coast.\nMangroves filter pollutants and purify water quality.\nThey provide carbon storage and act as buffers against waves.\nTherefore mangroves have important ecological and conservation value." },
      { q: "Write down the taxonomic hierarchy from the largest to the smallest, and explain the definition of a species.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Taxonomic hierarchy: kingdom → phylum → class → order → family → genus → species.\nThe species is the smallest taxonomic unit.\nDefinition of a species: a group of organisms that can interbreed naturally and produce fertile offspring.\nBinomial nomenclature: the scientific name consists of the genus name + species name." },
      { q: "Explain the effects of habitat destruction on biodiversity, giving an example in Hong Kong.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Habitat destruction reduces the habitats of organisms, decreasing the number of species.\nHabitat fragmentation divides populations, reducing gene flow, and the populations are more likely to become extinct.\nIt disrupts food chains and ecological balance.\nExamples: land clearing (removing rural vegetation), land reclamation, urbanisation, cutting down mangroves.\nProtecting natural habitats is the most fundamental way to conserve biodiversity." },
      { q: "Write down the main differences between protists, fungi and plants.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Protists: mostly single-celled eukaryotes (e.g. algae, amoeba); some can carry out photosynthesis.\nFungi: eukaryotic, no chloroplasts, cannot photosynthesise, obtain nutrients by absorption; have a cell wall (made of chitin), no roots, stems or leaves.\nPlants: eukaryotic, with chloroplasts able to photosynthesise (autotrophic), have a cell wall (made of cellulose), and generally have roots, stems and leaves.\nThe classification is based on cell structure, nutrition, etc." },
      { q: "Explain why scientists estimate that there are still many undiscovered species in the world.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Many habitats (deep sea, tropical rainforests) have not been fully explored.\nMicro-organisms and small organisms (microbes, insects) are extremely numerous and hard to record completely.\nTaxonomic research requires time and resources, and new species continue to be discovered.\nTherefore the total number of species in the world may be far greater than the number recorded." },
      { q: "Write down two endangered species in Hong Kong and the reasons why they are threatened.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Chinese white dolphin: affected by marine traffic, noise, pollution and habitat destruction.\nGreen sea turtle: breeding beaches are destroyed, entangled in fishing nets, pollution.\n(Others such as the black-faced spoonbill: loss of wetland habitats.)\nConservation measures: setting up marine parks, protecting breeding sites, legislation." },
      { q: "Explain why protecting natural habitats is more effective than artificial rearing in conserving biodiversity.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Natural habitats preserve complete ecosystems (food chains, habitats, species interactions).\nIn-situ conservation allows species to live and reproduce in natural conditions, maintaining genetic diversity.\nEx-situ conservation (zoos) protects only individual species, has limited space, and cannot preserve complete ecosystems.\nTherefore protecting natural habitats is the most fundamental and effective way to conserve biodiversity." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 30,
    name: "Origin of Life and Evidence of Evolution",
    questions: [
      { q: "Explain why the smaller the number of amino acid differences between organisms, the closer their evolutionary relationship.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The amino acid sequence of a polypeptide depends on the DNA base sequence.\nThe differences in amino acid sequences are caused by mutations in the DNA base sequences.\nOrganisms that are closely related evolutionarily have more similar DNA base sequences.\nTherefore their polypeptides have fewer amino acid differences." },
      { q: "Describe the limitations of the fossil record, and explain why fossils cannot fully record the history of biological evolution.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The fossil record has gaps; not all species have fossils.\nSome organisms have soft bodies and cannot form fossils.\nThe environments in which some organisms' remains are found are not suitable for fossil formation.\nFossils may be destroyed by natural changes, or lie in places difficult for humans to explore." },
      { q: "Describe the Miller–Urey experiment, and explain its significance to the study of the origin of life.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "The experiment simulated the early Earth's atmosphere (methane, ammonia, hydrogen, water vapour) and added energy (electric discharge) to simulate lightning.\nThe result was the production of organic molecules such as amino acids.\nIt shows that under the conditions of the early Earth, organic molecules could form from inorganic substances.\nIt supports the hypothesis of the origin of life: the inorganic substances in the primitive atmosphere interacted to first form organic molecules, which then combined into the most primitive organisms.\nBut the experiment did not produce complete cells or proteins." },
      { q: "Describe Pasteur's swan-necked flask experiment, and explain how it disproved spontaneous generation.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Pasteur put broth into a swan-necked flask and boiled it to kill the micro-organisms.\nThe swan neck allowed air to enter but not micro-organisms (they settled in the curved part of the neck).\nThe broth remained clear (no micro-organisms), showing that micro-organisms are not produced spontaneously.\nAfter breaking the neck to let micro-organisms enter, the broth went bad.\nThis proved that life can only come from existing life (biogenesis) and disproved spontaneous generation." },
      { q: "Write down the evidence of evolution (fossils, comparative anatomy, comparative embryology, comparative molecular biology).", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Fossils: fossils in older rock layers have simpler structures, showing that organisms change over time.\nComparative anatomy: homologous structures (same origin but possibly different functions) show a common ancestor, e.g. the wing of a bat and the arm of a human.\nComparative embryology: embryos of different species are similar in the early stage of development, showing a common ancestor.\nComparative molecular biology: the more similar the DNA base sequences or protein amino acid sequences, the closer the evolutionary relationship.\nVestigial organs (e.g. the human appendix) are also evidence of evolution." },
      { q: "Explain the difference between homologous structures and analogous structures, giving one example of each.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Homologous structures: have the same origin (embryonic origin) but possibly different functions, showing a common ancestor, e.g. the wing of a bat and the arm of a human.\nAnalogous structures: have the same function but different origins, not showing a common ancestor, e.g. the wing of a bird and the wing of an insect.\nHomologous structures support evolution; analogous structures reflect adaptation to similar environments (convergent evolution)." },
      { q: "Explain why marsupials in Australia differ from mammals on other continents.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Australia has been geographically isolated from other continents for a long time.\nAfter isolation, the marsupials and the placental mammals of other continents evolved independently.\nBoth developed similar adaptations to similar ecological niches (convergent evolution), but with different origins.\nGeographical isolation led to different evolutionary pathways, forming endemic species.\nThis is biogeographical evidence supporting evolution." },
      { q: "Explain how radiometric dating can determine the age of fossils.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Radioactive isotopes in rocks decay into stable isotopes at a fixed rate.\nMeasure the ratio of the parent isotope to the daughter isotope.\nAccording to the half-life (the time for half to decay), the age of the fossil/rock can be calculated.\nThis provides the absolute age of fossils, helping to arrange the evolutionary sequence." },
      { q: "Write down the conditions for fossil formation, and explain why organisms with soft bodies are unlikely to form fossils.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Conditions for fossil formation: the organism is quickly covered by sediment after death; it has hard parts (bones, shells); the environment is suitable for preservation; it is mineralised over a long time.\nThe soft bodies of organisms are easily decomposed by decomposers and bacteria after death, leaving no remains.\nTherefore soft-bodied organisms have a low chance of forming fossils, and the fossil record is incomplete." },
      { q: "Explain why comparing the DNA base sequences of different organisms can deduce their evolutionary relationships.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "DNA carries genetic information and controls the traits of organisms.\nClosely related organisms have a more recent common ancestor, accumulated fewer mutations, and have smaller DNA sequence differences.\nDistan­tly related organisms have accumulated more mutations and have larger DNA sequence differences.\nTherefore comparing DNA base sequences (or protein amino acid sequences) can deduce evolutionary relationships." },
      { q: "Write down the differences between the early Earth's atmosphere and the present atmosphere, and explain their significance to the origin of life.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The early atmosphere lacked oxygen and contained reducing gases such as methane, ammonia, hydrogen and water vapour.\nThe present atmosphere is rich in oxygen and nitrogen.\nUnder the early conditions, inorganic molecules could form organic molecules under energy (lightning, ultraviolet) (supported by the Miller experiment).\nOrganic molecules then combined to form the most primitive organisms; therefore the early atmosphere was favourable to the origin of life." },
      { q: "Explain why comparative embryology supports the theory of a common ancestor.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "The embryos of different vertebrates (fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds, mammals) are similar in the early stage of development (e.g. gill slits, tails).\nThe similarity suggests that they may have come from a common ancestor.\nEmbryonic development reflects a shared evolutionary history.\n(Homologous structures and molecular evidence also support this.)" },
      { q: "Write down the difference between 'biogenesis' and 'spontaneous generation', and explain why biogenesis is widely accepted.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Spontaneous generation: life can arise naturally from non-living matter.\nBiogenesis: life can only come from existing life.\nPasteur's swan-necked flask experiment proved that broth does not spontaneously produce micro-organisms, supporting biogenesis.\nTherefore biogenesis is widely accepted and spontaneous generation is disproved." },
      { q: "Explain why the fossil record has gaps, but it is still important evidence of evolution.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The fossil record has gaps because not all organisms can form fossils, fossils may be destroyed, or lie in places difficult to explore.\nBut fossils show the trend of organisms changing over time (fossils in older rocks have simpler structures).\nTransitional fossils (e.g. Archaeopteryx) show transitional features between organisms.\nTogether with other evidence (anatomy, embryology, molecular biology), fossils remain important evidence of evolution." },
      { q: "Compare the advantages of 'comparative molecular biology' and 'comparative anatomy' as evidence of evolution.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Molecular biology: compares DNA or protein sequences, quantitative and objective, can compare organisms with very different morphologies, and shows how close the relationships are.\nAnatomy: compares homologous structures, directly showing the common ancestor and structural homology, but is applicable only to organisms whose morphology can be compared.\nThe two complement each other; molecular evidence can supplement morphological evidence to deduce evolutionary relationships more accurately." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 31,
    name: "Mechanism of Evolution and Speciation",
    questions: [
      { q: "According to the modern understanding of evolution, explain Darwin's view of how giraffes evolved long necks.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Genetic variation exists in the giraffe population; some have long necks and others short.\nLong-necked individuals are better able to obtain food than short-necked ones, so they get more food.\nLong-necked individuals have a higher chance of surviving and reproducing.\nTherefore over several generations the proportion of long-necked individuals in the population gradually increases." },
      { q: "Explain why bacteria develop resistance to antibiotics.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Genetic variation exists in the bacterial population; some bacteria are resistant to antibiotics because of mutation.\nThe use of antibiotics kills the non-resistant bacteria.\nThe resistant bacteria are better adapted in an environment containing antibiotics, so they have a higher chance of survival.\nThey have a greater chance of reproducing, passing the favourable trait (resistance gene) to their offspring.\nAfter several generations the proportion of resistant bacteria rises and the antibiotic gradually becomes ineffective." },
      { q: "Write down the main concepts of Darwin's theory of natural selection.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Overproduction: organisms produce far more offspring than the environment can support.\nThe population size is roughly stable: individuals compete for resources.\nVariation exists between individuals.\nStruggle for existence: resources are limited and individuals compete to survive.\nSurvival of the fittest: individuals with favourable variations are more likely to survive and reproduce.\nFavourable traits are inherited by offspring: over many generations, the proportion of favourable genes in the population rises." },
      { q: "Explain how industrial melanism demonstrates natural selection.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Before the industrial revolution, light-coloured peppered moths were less easily caught by birds on light tree trunks, so they were numerous; dark moths were easily spotted and were few.\nIndustrial pollution made the tree trunks dark, so dark moths were less easily caught on dark trunks and survived better; light moths were easily caught.\nDark moths had a higher chance of survival and reproduction, passing the dark gene to their offspring.\nAfter several generations, dark moths became the majority in polluted areas.\nThis shows that the environment selects favourable traits (natural selection)." },
      { q: "Explain the difference between genetic drift and natural selection.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Genetic drift: random fluctuations in gene frequencies in a population, especially significant in small populations, not related to fitness.\nNatural selection: the environment selects traits favourable for survival and reproduction, increasing the frequency of favourable genes; it is directional.\nGenetic drift is random and directionless; natural selection is directional and related to adaptation.\nBoth can change the gene frequencies of a population (evolution)." },
      { q: "Write down the process of speciation (geographical isolation → reproductive isolation).", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Geographical isolation: the population is separated by geographical barriers (mountains, rivers), so they cannot interbreed and there is no gene flow.\nDifferent groups face different environments, and natural selection and mutation accumulate different variations.\nOver a long time, the genetic differences increase.\nWhen the differences accumulate to the point where they cannot interbreed, or breeding cannot produce fertile offspring, reproductive isolation arises.\nDifferent species are then formed." },
      { q: "Explain why sexual reproduction promotes evolution more than asexual reproduction.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Sexual reproduction involves meiosis (independent assortment, crossing over) and random fertilisation, producing genetic recombination.\nThe offspring have more genetic variation.\nVariation provides the raw material for natural selection, so the population is better able to adapt to environmental changes.\nAsexual reproduction produces genetically identical offspring, and variation comes only from mutation, so evolution is slower.\nTherefore sexual reproduction promotes evolution." },
      { q: "Explain why mutation is the raw material of evolution, but mutation itself does not directly determine the direction of evolution.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Mutation produces new alleles, which is the source of genetic variation and provides the material for natural selection.\nMutations are random, and most are neutral or harmful.\nNatural selection determines which variations are retained (favourable) or eliminated (harmful).\nTherefore mutation provides variation, while natural selection determines the direction of evolution." },
      { q: "Write down the definition of a 'species', and explain the importance of reproductive isolation.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Definition of a species: a group of organisms that can interbreed naturally and produce fertile offspring.\nReproductive isolation: different groups cannot interbreed, or breeding cannot produce fertile offspring.\nReproductive isolation prevents gene exchange (no gene flow) between groups.\nWhen reproductive isolation is established, the groups evolve separately and become different species.\nReproductive isolation marks the formation of a species." },
      { q: "Explain why natural selection acts directly on the phenotype rather than the genotype.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Natural selection acts on the phenotype (observable characteristics) of individuals, because the phenotype determines the individual's interaction with the environment.\nIndividuals with favourable phenotypes have a higher chance of surviving and reproducing.\nThe genes (genotype) carried by these individuals are thereby retained and passed on.\nTherefore natural selection changes the gene frequencies of a population indirectly through the phenotype." },
      { q: "Write down how gene flow and genetic drift affect the gene composition of a population.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Gene flow: genes move between populations through the migration of individuals, increasing the similarity of gene frequencies between populations and reducing genetic differences.\nGenetic drift: random fluctuations of gene frequencies in small populations, which can make some genes disappear or become fixed.\nBoth change the gene frequencies of a population and affect genetic diversity and evolution.\nGeographical isolation blocks gene flow and promotes speciation." },
      { q: "Explain why the appearance of resistant bacteria shows that evolution can occur in a short time.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Bacteria reproduce quickly and have short generations, producing many offspring in a short time.\nMutation produces resistant variants, and antibiotics select the resistant bacteria.\nThe resistant bacteria reproduce rapidly, and the gene frequencies of the population change significantly within a few generations.\nTherefore a bacterial population can evolve antibiotic resistance in a short time, showing that evolution is an ongoing process." },
      { q: "Compare Darwin's natural selection with Lamarck's use-and-disuse theory.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Darwin: variation exists in the population, the environment selects favourable variations, and favourable traits are inherited by offspring.\nLamarck: traits acquired by an individual through use or disuse (acquired characteristics) are inherited by offspring.\nDarwin's theory is supported by modern genetics; Lamarck's inheritance of acquired characteristics has been disproved.\nExample (giraffe): Darwin — long-neck variation is selected; Lamarck — the neck becomes long through stretching and is inherited." },
      { q: "Explain why natural selection still operates when the environment is stable.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Even when the environment is stable, genetic variation exists between individuals.\nMutations continue to produce new variations.\nIndividuals compete for resources, and those best adapted to the current environment survive and reproduce (stabilising selection).\nThe gene frequencies of the population adjust to best suit the environment, but mutations continue to provide new variation, so evolution can still occur." },
      { q: "Write down four factors that affect the gene frequencies of a population.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Natural selection: the environment selects favourable genes.\nGene mutation: produces new alleles.\nGene flow: migration of individuals introduces or removes genes.\nGenetic drift: random fluctuations of gene frequencies in small populations.\n(Non-random mating can also have an effect.) These factors jointly determine the evolution of a population." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 32,
    name: "Regulation of Body Temperature",
    questions: [
      { q: "Explain why the blood flow to the skin increases during exercise.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Muscle contraction produces heat.\nThe temperature receptors in the hypothalamus/skin detect a rise in body temperature.\nThe heat-loss centre of the hypothalamus is stimulated and sends out nerve impulses.\nThis causes the small arteries near the skin surface to dilate, increasing the blood flow near the skin surface to promote heat loss." },
      { q: "Explain why, in hot and humid weather, the efficiency of heat loss of the body decreases and the perceived temperature is higher.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Higher relative humidity hinders the evaporation of sweat.\nAs a result, heat cannot be effectively lost to the surroundings through the evaporation of sweat.\nTherefore a sensation of higher temperature is produced.\nAt higher temperatures, the gradient between body temperature and air temperature narrows, so the efficiency of heat loss by conduction/convection/radiation is also lower." },
      { q: "Write down the location of the body temperature regulating centre in humans, and describe the body's responses in the cold.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "The body temperature regulating centre is in the hypothalamus.\nCold responses: the small arteries in the skin constrict, reducing skin blood flow to reduce heat loss; the arrector pili muscles contract so the hairs stand erect, forming a thicker layer of still air; the sweat glands reduce sweating; the skeletal muscles shiver to produce heat; a long-term response is that thyroxine secretion increases, raising the metabolic rate to produce heat." },
      { q: "Describe the heat-loss responses of the body in hot weather.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The small arteries in the skin dilate, so more blood flows near the skin surface and more heat is lost.\nThe arrector pili muscles relax, the hairs lie flat, and the still-air layer becomes thinner.\nThe sweat glands increase sweating, and the evaporation of sweat carries away heat.\nMuscle activity is reduced to lower heat production.\nBody temperature regulation is a negative feedback mechanism." },
      { q: "Explain why sweating helps lose heat, and write down the factors affecting the rate of evaporation of sweat.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Sweat is mainly water; when it evaporates it absorbs heat from the skin (latent heat of evaporation), carrying away heat and cooling the body.\nFactors affecting evaporation: environmental temperature, humidity, wind speed, surface area of the skin and amount of sweat.\nHigh humidity slows evaporation; high wind speed speeds it up.\nSweating too much without replacing water can cause dehydration." },
      { q: "Compare the four routes of heat transfer: conduction, convection, radiation and evaporation.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Conduction: direct transfer between two objects in contact (e.g. losing heat when the body touches a cold object).\nConvection: transfer between an object and moving water or air (e.g. wind blowing over the skin loses heat).\nRadiation: transfer of infrared radiation between objects not in contact (e.g. the Sun's radiation warming the body).\nEvaporation: heat is lost when liquid water changes to vapour (e.g. evaporation of sweat).\nBody temperature regulation maintains a constant temperature by regulating heat exchange through these routes." },
      { q: "Explain why shivering helps keep warm in cold weather.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "In the cold, the skeletal muscles contract rapidly and involuntarily (shivering).\nMuscle contraction carries out respiration, releasing a large amount of heat.\nShivering produces heat and raises the body temperature; it is one of the heat-producing responses.\n(Long-term heat production relies on thyroxine raising the metabolic rate.)" },
      { q: "Explain the role of thyroxine in body temperature regulation, and why this is a long-term response.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "In the cold, the hypothalamus stimulates the pituitary to secrete thyroid-stimulating hormone, which makes the thyroid secrete more thyroxine.\nThyroxine raises the metabolic rate of cells, increasing heat production.\nSince hormones are transported by the blood and act slowly but persistently, this is a long-term adaptation.\nIt helps the body maintain its body temperature in a persistently cold environment." },
      { q: "Describe how the skin acts as both a receptor and an effector in body temperature regulation.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The skin contains temperature receptors that detect changes in skin surface temperature and send information to the hypothalamus.\nThe skin is also an effector: the sweat glands (sweating to regulate heat loss), the small arteries in the skin (regulating blood flow to lose or retain heat), and the arrector pili muscles (regulating the insulating layer).\nThe hypothalamus integrates the information and controls the effectors in the skin through nerves.\nThe skin therefore both senses and executes regulation." },
      { q: "Explain why the body temperature rises during exercise, and how the body brings it back to normal.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "During exercise, muscle contraction carries out respiration, releasing a large amount of heat, so the body temperature rises.\nThe hypothalamus detects the rise in blood temperature and stimulates the heat-loss centre.\nThe small arteries in the skin dilate, increasing blood flow to lose heat.\nThe sweat glands increase sweating, and the evaporation of sweat carries away heat.\nThe body temperature returns to the normal range (negative feedback)." },
      { q: "Write down the causes of heat stroke, and explain why it is dangerous.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Causes of heat stroke: the evaporation of sweat cannot effectively remove heat (low wind speed, high humidity, dehydration preventing sweating); convection and radiation are blocked; when the environmental temperature is higher than the body temperature, the body gains heat from the environment.\nIn heat stroke the body temperature is too high, beyond the normal regulatory range, and temperature regulation fails.\nHigh body temperature damages the brain and other organs (proteins are denatured), and can be fatal.\nImmediate cooling and medical treatment are needed." },
      { q: "Explain why body temperature regulation is a negative feedback mechanism.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "When the body temperature deviates from normal, an opposite response is produced to bring it back.\nBody temperature high → heat-loss responses (vasodilation, sweating).\nBody temperature low → heat-production and heat-retention responses (vasoconstriction, shivering).\nThe response stops after the deviation is corrected, so it is negative feedback." },
      { q: "Compare warm-blooded and cold-blooded animals in terms of body temperature regulation.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Warm-blooded animals (birds, mammals): can maintain a relatively stable body temperature, regulating it by heat production and loss (metabolic heat production, shivering, sweating, regulating blood flow); not affected by the environmental temperature, so their activities are not limited by the environment.\nCold-blooded animals (fish, reptiles): their body temperature changes with the environment and they rely on external heat; they become less active in cold weather (hibernation).\nWarm-blooded animals consume more energy to maintain a constant temperature." },
      { q: "Explain why exercising in humid weather is more uncomfortable than in dry weather.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "In humid weather the humidity is high and sweat evaporates slowly.\nLess heat is carried away by evaporation, so the efficiency of heat loss is low.\nThe body cannot lose heat effectively, the body temperature rises, and one feels hot and uncomfortable.\nSweating is heavy but evaporation is slow, so sweat accumulates, and at the same time water and salts are lost, increasing the risk of dehydration." },
      { q: "An athlete exercised for a long time in very hot weather.\n(a) Write down the heat-loss responses of his body.\n(b) What should he pay attention to in order to prevent heat stroke?", marks: "4 marks", answer: "(a) The skin blood vessels dilate, sweating increases greatly to lose heat, and the heart rate rises to carry heat to the skin.\n(b) Replenish water and electrolytes (sports drinks), avoid prolonged exposure to the sun, exercise at cooler times, wear light and breathable clothing, rest at intervals, and stop exercising if feeling unwell.\n(Prevent dehydration and overheating.)" }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 33,
    name: "Regulation of Water Content",
    questions: [
      { q: "Explain why the volume of urine increases after drinking a large amount of water.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "After drinking a lot of water, the water potential of the blood rises.\nThe osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus detect the rise in the blood water potential.\nThey stimulate the pituitary to release less ADH.\nAs the ADH level in the blood falls, the permeability of the collecting duct wall to water decreases.\nA smaller proportion of water is reabsorbed into the blood, so a large amount of dilute urine is formed to excrete the excess water." },
      { q: "Explain why diabetic patients urinate more frequently than healthy people.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The patients cannot reabsorb all the glucose from the glomerular filtrate, so some glucose remains in the filtrate.\nTherefore, compared with healthy people, the glomerular filtrate has a lower water potential.\nAs a result, the collecting duct can reabsorb a smaller proportion of water.\nThe patients produce a large volume of urine and need to urinate more frequently." },
      { q: "Describe the formation of urine (ultrafiltration and reabsorption).", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Ultrafiltration: the high blood pressure in the glomerulus forces water and small molecules (glucose, amino acids, salts, urea) out of the blood into Bowman's capsule, forming the glomerular filtrate; the filtrate does not contain blood cells or large protein molecules.\nReabsorption: glucose and amino acids are completely reabsorbed by active transport in the proximal convoluted tubule; most salts are reabsorbed; water is reabsorbed by osmosis; some urea diffuses back into the blood.\nMore than 99% of the filtrate is reabsorbed, and the remainder forms urine." },
      { q: "Explain how ADH (antidiuretic hormone) regulates the water balance.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "When the body is short of water, the blood water potential falls, detected by the osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus, which stimulate the pituitary to release more ADH.\nADH increases the permeability of the collecting duct wall to water, so a larger proportion of water is reabsorbed, forming a small volume of concentrated urine.\nAfter drinking a lot of water the blood water potential rises, less ADH is released, the permeability of the collecting duct decreases, and a large volume of dilute urine is formed.\nThrough negative feedback, the blood water potential is maintained stable.\n(Alcohol/caffeine inhibits ADH release, causing frequent urination.)" },
      { q: "Write down the other functions of the kidneys (besides excretion), and explain how urea is produced.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The kidneys regulate the balance of water and salts in the body; excrete nitrogenous waste (urea); secrete hormones (such as erythropoietin) to regulate the production of red blood cells.\nUrea comes from the deamination of amino acids in the liver: the amino group of excess amino acids is removed and converted into urea.\nUrea is transported by the blood to the kidneys and excreted in urine." },
      { q: "Explain why the glomerular filtrate does not contain protein and blood cells.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The pores of the glomerular filtration membrane (the capillary wall and the inner wall of Bowman's capsule) are small.\nBlood cells and plasma proteins are large molecules that cannot pass through the filtration membrane.\nOnly water and small molecules (glucose, amino acids, salts, urea) can pass through.\nIf protein appears in the urine, it shows that the glomeruli are damaged (the permeability of the filtration membrane is abnormal)." },
      { q: "Describe the principle of renal dialysis, and explain why the dialysis fluid needs specific components.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Renal dialysis uses a semipermeable membrane; the blood flows through the dialyser, and wastes (urea) and excess water diffuse into the dialysis fluid.\nThe dialysis fluid has a composition similar to normal plasma (containing appropriate concentrations of glucose and salts) to maintain the concentration gradient.\nIf the dialysis fluid contained urea, urea would not be removed; the dialysis fluid contains no urea, so urea in the blood diffuses into the dialysis fluid.\nWastes in the blood are removed by diffusion, replacing the function of the kidneys." },
      { q: "Compare normal urine and glomerular filtrate in terms of composition.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The glomerular filtrate contains glucose and amino acids (which are reabsorbed, so normal urine does not contain them).\nThe filtrate contains a large amount of water and salts; urine contains less water and salts (most are reabsorbed).\nUrine contains urea (some urea remains in the urine).\nNormal urine does not contain protein, blood cells or glucose; the filtrate does not contain protein or blood cells.\nReabsorption makes the compositions of the two different." },
      { q: "Explain why the urine volume decreases in high temperature or after vigorous exercise.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "In high temperature or after vigorous exercise, a lot of water is lost through sweating, and the body is short of water.\nThe water potential of the blood falls (dehydration), detected by the osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus.\nThe pituitary releases more ADH, increasing the permeability of the collecting duct to water, so more water is reabsorbed.\nTherefore the urine volume decreases to retain water; at the same time thirst prompts drinking." },
      { q: "Write down the components of the urinary system, and describe the pathway of urine from the kidneys to discharge.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Urinary system: a pair of kidneys, two ureters, one urinary bladder, one urethra.\nPathway of urine: kidney → ureter → bladder (storage, controlled by sphincters) → urethra → out of the body.\nThe kidneys form urine, the ureters transport it, the bladder stores it, and the urethra discharges it." },
      { q: "Explain why the urine volume increases after drinking alcohol.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Alcohol inhibits the release of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) by the pituitary.\nAs the ADH level falls, the permeability of the collecting duct wall to water decreases.\nLess water is reabsorbed back into the blood.\nTherefore a large amount of urine is produced and the urine volume increases (diuresis), which may lead to dehydration." },
      { q: "A patient's urine contains a large amount of protein.\n(a) Deduce the possible cause.\n(b) What is the effect on the body?", marks: "4 marks", answer: "(a) The glomeruli are damaged (e.g. glomerulonephritis), increasing the permeability of the filtration membrane, so protein leaks into the filtrate and cannot be reabsorbed.\n(b) A large amount of protein is lost in the urine, so the body loses protein.\nThe decrease in plasma proteins lowers the water potential of the plasma, reducing the return of tissue fluid, causing oedema.\nMedical treatment is needed." },
      { q: "Describe the functions of the parts of a nephron (Bowman's capsule, proximal convoluted tubule, loop of Henle, distal convoluted tubule, collecting duct).", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Bowman's capsule: collects the filtrate formed by ultrafiltration in the glomerulus.\nProximal convoluted tubule: reabsorbs glucose, amino acids and most salts (active transport); some water is reabsorbed by osmosis.\nLoop of Henle: establishes the concentration gradient of the medulla, helping to concentrate urine.\nDistal convoluted tubule: regulates the reabsorption of salts and water (affected by hormones).\nCollecting duct: regulates water reabsorption under the effect of ADH, concentrating the urine." },
      { q: "Explain why the renal vein contains less urea and oxygen than the renal artery.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The kidney filters the blood and excretes urea (urea enters the urine), so the renal vein contains less urea.\nThe kidney cells carry out aerobic respiration and consume oxygen, so the renal vein contains less oxygen.\nThe renal artery supplies oxygen and nutrients; the renal vein carries away less urea and oxygen.\n(The renal vein contains more carbon dioxide.)" },
      { q: "Write down the function of aldosterone, and explain how it helps maintain the water balance.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Aldosterone is secreted by the adrenal gland and promotes the reabsorption of sodium ions in the nephron (distal convoluted tubule).\nAs sodium ions are reabsorbed, the change in water potential causes water to be reabsorbed by osmosis along with the sodium ions.\nWater is retained in the body, maintaining the blood volume and blood pressure.\nWhen aldosterone is insufficient, sodium and water are lost and blood pressure falls." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 34,
    name: "Regulation of Gas Content in the Blood",
    questions: [
      { q: "Explain how an increase in the carbon dioxide concentration in the blood increases the rate and depth of breathing.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Body cells continuously produce carbon dioxide through respiration, and more is produced during exercise.\nThe accumulation of carbon dioxide in the blood lowers the pH of the blood/cerebrospinal fluid.\nThe chemoreceptors in the carotid body, aortic body and medulla oblongata detect the high carbon dioxide concentration.\nThese receptors send nerve impulses to stimulate the respiratory centre in the medulla oblongata.\nThe medulla sends more impulses to the respiratory muscles (intercostal muscles and diaphragm), making them contract faster and more strongly, so the rate and depth of breathing increase." },
      { q: "Explain why a marathon runner cannot run the whole distance at the average speed of a 100 m runner.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "The marathon runner has to run a much longer distance, so the muscles must keep contracting for a long time.\nIf he ran the marathon at the speed of a 100 m race, the oxygen supply to the muscles would be insufficient.\nThe muscles would carry out anaerobic respiration and produce lactic acid.\nThe lactic acid would accumulate in the muscles.\nThis causes muscle fatigue, and the muscles can no longer contract." },
      { q: "Describe the transport of oxygen in the blood, and the forms of transport of carbon dioxide.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Oxygen: in the lungs it combines with haemoglobin in red blood cells to form oxyhaemoglobin, which releases oxygen in the tissues.\nCarbon dioxide: dissolved in plasma (about 5%); combined with haemoglobin to form carbaminohaemoglobin (about 10%); reacting with water to form hydrogencarbonate ions (about 85%).\nCarbon dioxide is mostly transported as hydrogencarbonate ions." },
      { q: "Explain the location of the respiratory centre and the mechanism of its regulation (with carbon dioxide as the main stimulus).", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Breathing is controlled by the respiratory centre in the medulla oblongata, which sets the basic rhythm and regulates the rate and depth of breathing.\nThe blood carbon dioxide concentration is the main regulating factor, not the oxygen concentration.\nA rise in carbon dioxide lowers the blood pH, stimulating the chemoreceptors in the carotid body, aortic body and medulla oblongata.\nThe receptors send impulses to stimulate the respiratory centre, increasing the rate and depth of breathing.\n(The brain can control breathing voluntarily, but the basic rhythm is maintained by the medulla.)" },
      { q: "Explain why hyperventilation may cause dizziness.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Hyperventilation removes too much carbon dioxide.\nThe blood carbon dioxide concentration becomes too low, and the blood becomes alkaline (pH rises).\nAlkalosis affects nerve function, constricts blood vessels and reduces blood flow to the brain.\nTherefore symptoms such as dizziness and tingling in the hands and feet appear.\n(Breathing is regulated mainly by carbon dioxide; hyperventilation disturbs the acid–base balance.)" },
      { q: "Explain the cause and harm of carbon monoxide poisoning.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Carbon monoxide has a much higher affinity for haemoglobin than oxygen (about 200 times).\nCarbon monoxide combines with haemoglobin to form carboxyhaemoglobin, occupying the oxygen-carrying sites.\nHaemoglobin can no longer carry oxygen, and the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood falls greatly.\nThe tissues are deprived of oxygen; in severe cases one may become unconscious or even die; carbon monoxide is colourless and odourless and is not easily noticed." },
      { q: "Explain the role of carbonic anhydrase in the transport of carbon dioxide.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "In red blood cells, carbon dioxide reacts with water to form carbonic acid (CO₂ + H₂O → H₂CO₃).\nCarbonic anhydrase catalyses this reaction, making it proceed rapidly.\nCarbonic acid dissociates into hydrogen ions and hydrogencarbonate ions.\nMost hydrogencarbonate ions diffuse into the plasma, and chloride ions move into the red blood cells to maintain electrical neutrality; in the lungs the reaction is reversed, releasing carbon dioxide." },
      { q: "Explain why the body increases the number of red blood cells at high altitude.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "At high altitude the air is thin and the oxygen partial pressure is low.\nThe oxygen content of the blood is low, and the body detects hypoxia.\nThe kidneys secrete erythropoietin, stimulating the bone marrow to produce more red blood cells.\nThe number of red blood cells and the haemoglobin level increase, raising the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood and helping to adapt to the low-oxygen environment." },
      { q: "Explain why oxyhaemoglobin releases oxygen in the tissues.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Tissue cells carry out respiration and consume oxygen, so the oxygen partial pressure in the tissues is low.\nOxyhaemoglobin combines with oxygen at high oxygen partial pressure (in the lungs) and releases it at low oxygen partial pressure (in the tissues).\nIn active tissues the temperature is high and the carbon dioxide concentration is high (pH low), so the affinity of haemoglobin for oxygen decreases (Bohr effect) and more oxygen is released.\nTherefore the tissues obtain enough oxygen for respiration." },
      { q: "Describe the pacemaker (sinoatrial node) of the heart and the cardiac cycle.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "The sinoatrial node (pacemaker) is located in the wall of the right atrium and sends out electrical impulses.\nThe impulses spread through the atrial wall, causing the atria to contract.\nThe impulses are conducted to the atrioventricular node and then to the ventricular wall, starting ventricular contraction.\nCardiac cycle: atrial contraction → ventricular contraction → atrial and ventricular relaxation.\nDuring ventricular contraction the blood pressure is higher than in the arteries and the semilunar valves open; during relaxation the semilunar valves close (producing heart sounds)." },
      { q: "Explain how the cardiac output increases during exercise (nervous and hormonal control).", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Nervous control: during exercise the blood pH falls or the blood pressure falls, stimulating the cardio-acceleratory centre of the cardiovascular centre, which through sympathetic nerves makes the heart muscle contract faster and more strongly, so the cardiac output rises.\nHormonal control: the sympathetic nerves stimulate the adrenal gland to secrete adrenaline, which makes the heart muscle contract faster and more strongly.\nCardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume.\nDuring exercise the cardiac output increases, promoting the transport of oxygen to the skeletal muscles and speeding up the removal of carbon dioxide." },
      { q: "Explain why the breathing rate remains relatively high for a period after exercise.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "During exercise the muscles carry out anaerobic respiration, producing and accumulating lactic acid (oxygen debt).\nAfter exercise, extra oxygen is needed to break down the lactic acid (converting it into carbon dioxide and water, or glycogen).\nTherefore the breathing rate stays relatively high, supplying the extra oxygen to repay the oxygen debt.\nAfter the lactic acid is removed, breathing returns to normal." },
      { q: "Write down the receptors involved in the regulation of breathing and their locations.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Carotid body: located at the carotid artery, detecting the concentrations of carbon dioxide (and oxygen, pH) in the blood.\nAortic body: located at the aorta, detecting the concentrations of carbon dioxide (and oxygen, pH) in the blood.\nChemoreceptors in the medulla oblongata: detect the pH of the cerebrospinal fluid (reflecting the carbon dioxide concentration).\nThese receptors send impulses to stimulate the respiratory centre, regulating breathing." },
      { q: "Explain why the blood carbon dioxide concentration, rather than the oxygen concentration, is the main regulating factor of breathing.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The carbon dioxide concentration changes directly with metabolic activity and reflects the body's need for ventilation.\nCarbon dioxide affects the blood pH (forming carbonic acid), and the pH change is easily detected by the receptors.\nEven if the oxygen concentration falls slightly, normal breathing is sufficient to supply it; only severe oxygen deficiency affects breathing.\nTherefore breathing is mainly regulated by the carbon dioxide concentration (to maintain the acid–base balance)." },
      { q: "An asthma patient has difficulty breathing during exercise.\n(a) Explain how asthma affects gas exchange.\n(b) Suggest two ways to improve it.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "(a) Asthma narrows the airways, causes inflammation and increases mucus, increasing airflow resistance and reducing ventilation; gas exchange in the alveoli decreases, so the blood oxygen falls and carbon dioxide rises.\n(b) Use a bronchodilator (inhaler) to widen the airways; use medication before exercise, avoid triggers (allergens), carry out breathing training, exercise progressively (any two)." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 35,
    name: "Hormonal Control of Reproductive Cycle",
    questions: [
      { q: "Explain how the progesterone in contraceptive pills helps women avoid pregnancy.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "A high level of progesterone inhibits the secretion of FSH and LH by the pituitary.\nThe lower level of FSH is not enough to stimulate the development of follicles.\nThe lower level of LH is not enough to stimulate ovulation.\nTherefore no egg is released.\nWithout an egg meeting a sperm, fertilisation cannot occur." },
      { q: "To investigate the relationship between oestrogen and FSH, a group of women received extra injections of oestrogen. Explain the effect of oestrogen on FSH, and the function of the oestrogen in contraceptive pills.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "During the period of oestrogen injection, FSH remained at a low level.\nAfter stopping the oestrogen injection, the FSH level rose again.\nThis shows that oestrogen has a negative feedback effect on the FSH level / inhibits the production or secretion of FSH.\nThe oestrogen in contraceptive pills inhibits the production of FSH.\nTherefore no follicle grows.\nThus there is no mature egg for ovulation / no ovulation." },
      { q: "Describe the roles of the four main hormones (FSH, LH, oestrogen, progesterone) in the menstrual cycle.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "FSH (pituitary): stimulates the development of follicles and stimulates the follicles to secrete oestrogen.\nLH (pituitary): stimulates ovulation.\nOestrogen (from the developing follicle): stimulates the thickening of the uterine lining; the pre-ovulatory peak positively feeds back to stimulate LH.\nProgesterone (from the corpus luteum): maintains the thickness of the uterine lining, preparing for implantation.\nHigh levels of oestrogen and progesterone inhibit FSH and LH (negative feedback), preventing the development of another follicle." },
      { q: "Describe the three phases of the menstrual cycle (before ovulation, ovulation, after ovulation).", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Before ovulation (days 1–13): low levels of oestrogen and progesterone cause menstruation; FSH stimulates follicle development; the follicle secretes oestrogen which thickens the lining.\nOvulation (about day 14): the oestrogen peak stimulates a surge of LH (positive feedback), and the high LH stimulates ovulation.\nAfter ovulation (days 15–28): the corpus luteum secretes oestrogen and progesterone; progesterone maintains the lining and increases its blood supply; the high hormone levels inhibit FSH and LH, preventing the development of another follicle." },
      { q: "Explain the different outcomes of the menstrual cycle when fertilisation occurs and when it does not.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "No fertilisation: the corpus luteum degenerates, oestrogen and progesterone fall, the lining sheds → menstruation; the pituitary secretes FSH again and the cycle restarts.\nFertilisation: the embryo secretes human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG), preventing the degeneration of the corpus luteum; the corpus luteum continues to secrete oestrogen and progesterone to maintain the lining; the placenta later takes over hormone secretion.\nhCG is the indicator used in pregnancy tests." },
      { q: "Explain why the uterine lining thickens and its blood supply increases after ovulation.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "After ovulation the corpus luteum secretes progesterone (and oestrogen).\nProgesterone maintains and increases the thickness and blood supply of the uterine lining, making it vascular and secretory.\nThis prepares the lining for the implantation and development of the embryo, providing adequate nutrients and oxygen.\nIf not fertilised, the hormones fall and the lining sheds to form menstruation." },
      { q: "Write down the contraceptive principle of the contraceptive pill, and explain why it has side-effect risks.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "The pill contains synthetic oestrogen and progesterone, maintaining high levels to inhibit the secretion of FSH and LH → no follicle development, no ovulation.\nProgesterone also thickens the cervical mucus, preventing sperm from passing.\nSide-effect risks: the hormones alter the endocrine balance and may increase the risk of thrombosis and cardiovascular disease; they may cause nausea, breast tenderness, etc.\nThose with a history of cardiovascular disease or smokers should not use it; medical guidance is needed." },
      { q: "Compare the principle of the emergency contraceptive pill and the regular contraceptive pill.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Regular pill: taken continuously, synthetic oestrogen and progesterone suppress ovulation.\nEmergency pill: a high dose of progesterone taken shortly after intercourse, preventing/delaying ovulation, thickening the cervical mucus, and reducing the thickness of the lining to prevent implantation.\nThe emergency pill is only for emergencies and cannot be used regularly.\nBoth prevent pregnancy through hormonal action." },
      { q: "Explain why a pregnancy test strip can detect hCG in early pregnancy.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "After fertilisation, the embryo (trophoblast) secretes human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG).\nhCG enters the mother's blood and is excreted into the urine by the kidneys.\nThe pregnancy test strip detects hCG in the urine.\nIf hCG is detected, it shows that the embryo has implanted and is secreting hormones, i.e. pregnancy." },
      { q: "Write down the methods of treating infertility (hormone treatment, in vitro fertilisation).", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Hormone treatment: synthetic FSH/LH stimulate follicle development and ovulation; oestrogen and progesterone stimulate the thickening of the lining.\nIn vitro fertilisation (IVF): hormones stimulate the ovaries to collect several mature eggs; the eggs are fertilised with sperm outside the body to form embryos; healthy embryos are selected and transferred into the uterus.\nPreimplantation genetic testing (PGT) screens for genetic diseases.\nTreatment targets the cause of infertility (blocked oviducts, hormonal imbalance, etc.)." },
      { q: "Explain why the oestrogen peak is crucial to ovulation, and the difference between positive feedback and negative feedback.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Before ovulation the follicle secretes large amounts of oestrogen, which reaches a peak.\nThe high oestrogen level exerts positive feedback on the pituitary, stimulating a surge of LH.\nThe LH surge triggers ovulation (the follicle ruptures and releases an egg).\nAfter ovulation the oestrogen and progesterone levels are high, exerting negative feedback on the pituitary, inhibiting FSH and LH to prevent the development of another follicle.\nPositive feedback amplifies a response (oestrogen → LH → ovulation); negative feedback suppresses a response to maintain stability." },
      { q: "Describe the physiological changes during menopause.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "During menopause the ovarian function declines and the follicles are exhausted.\nThe secretion of oestrogen and progesterone drops greatly.\nThe FSH and LH levels rise (because negative feedback is removed).\nMenstruation becomes irregular and eventually stops (menopause).\nThe fall in oestrogen causes hot flushes, osteoporosis and other symptoms." },
      { q: "Explain why the menstrual cycle repeats regularly when there is no pregnancy.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "When there is no fertilisation, the corpus luteum degenerates and the levels of oestrogen and progesterone fall.\nThe uterine lining loses support and sheds, forming menstruation.\nThe fall in hormones removes the inhibition of FSH by the pituitary.\nThe pituitary secretes FSH again, stimulating the development of a new follicle and starting a new cycle.\nTherefore the menstrual cycle repeats regularly." },
      { q: "Write down the synergistic effect of progesterone and oestrogen on the uterine lining.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Oestrogen (before ovulation) stimulates the thickening and repair of the uterine lining.\nProgesterone (after ovulation) maintains the thickness of the lining, increases its blood supply and secretion, preparing it to receive the embryo.\nThe two work synergistically to make the lining suitable for implantation in the second half of the cycle.\nIf no embryo implants, the two fall and the lining sheds to form menstruation." },
      { q: "Explain why it is difficult to determine the fertile period when a woman's menstrual cycle is irregular.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The date of ovulation is not fixed, so the fertile period is hard to predict.\nThe 'safe period' method relies on predicting ovulation, which is impossible with an irregular cycle.\nSperm can survive in the reproductive tract for several days and the egg for about one day, so the fertile period spans the days before and after ovulation.\nTherefore, with an irregular cycle, contraception or pregnancy planning is hard to manage." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 36,
    name: "Human Impact on the Environment",
    questions: [
      { q: "Explain how 'eutrophication' leads to the death of fish in water.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "Fertilisers are leached into the water, causing algae to multiply rapidly.\nThe algae block sunlight, so aquatic plants at the bottom cannot get enough sunlight for photosynthesis and die, and the oxygen content of the water falls.\nAt night the algae carry out respiration, using up the oxygen in the water.\nWhen the nutrients are exhausted the algae die, and bacteria decompose the mass of algae, further consuming the oxygen in the water.\nAquatic organisms (fish) die from lack of oxygen (suffocation)." },
      { q: "Explain the causes of global warming and two of its effects.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Burning fossil fuels releases large amounts of greenhouse gases such as carbon dioxide.\nRadiation from the Sun reaches the Earth; the Earth's surface absorbs it and reflects heat (infrared radiation).\nThe greenhouse gases trap some of this infrared radiation, so less is radiated into space and the atmospheric temperature rises.\nEffect 1: glaciers and ice caps melt, sea levels rise, and low-lying areas may be flooded.\nEffect 2: abnormal weather, with more frequent heatwaves, droughts and heavy rain, and conditions favourable to the spread of infectious diseases." },
      { q: "Write down the causes of acid rain and its effects on the environment.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Causes: burning fossil fuels releases sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides, which dissolve in rain to form sulphuric acid and nitric acid.\nEffects: aquatic organisms are affected (acidification of water bodies); the cuticle of leaves is damaged and photosynthesis is inhibited; the soil pH falls and minerals are lost; buildings and sculptures are corroded.\nControl: reduce sulphur emissions (flue gas desulphurisation), use clean energy, reduce vehicle emissions." },
      { q: "Explain biomagnification, and illustrate with an example its effect on organisms at high trophic levels.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Biomagnification: toxic substances (such as DDT) are not easily broken down and are passed along the food chain, accumulating to higher concentrations at higher trophic levels.\nLower trophic level organisms take in small amounts of DDT; after being eaten by higher trophic levels, the toxin accumulates level by level.\nTop predators (such as birds of prey) eat many lower level organisms, so the DDT concentration in their bodies is high.\nEffects: damage to reproduction (thinning of egg shells), damage to the nervous system, decline of populations.\nThis explains why top predators are most affected by persistent pollutants." },
      { q: "Explain why population growth intensifies environmental problems.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "A larger population consumes more natural resources (food, water, energy, land).\nIt produces more waste and pollutants (sewage, solid waste, exhaust gases).\nMore land is needed, leading to land clearing, deforestation and land reclamation, destroying habitats.\nTherefore population growth intensifies resource consumption and environmental degradation (habitat destruction and pollution)." },
      { q: "Compare renewable and non-renewable resources, giving two examples of each.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Renewable resources: can be produced continuously by natural processes, e.g. solar energy, wind energy, hydro power, biomass.\nNon-renewable resources: limited supply and used up when consumed, e.g. coal, oil, natural gas, minerals.\nUsing non-renewable resources produces pollution and greenhouse gases.\nSustainable development should use more renewable resources and save non-renewable ones." },
      { q: "Explain the effects of overfishing on the marine ecology, and write down the measures for sustainable fisheries.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Overfishing: fish are caught faster than they can grow, so the number of fish declines.\nIt disrupts food chains and affects other species; biodiversity declines.\nDestructive fishing (trawling, fish bombing, cyanide) destroys habitats (such as coral reefs).\nSustainable measures: prohibit destructive fishing, set up closed seasons, limit the catch and net mesh size, aquaculture, artificial reef programmes." },
      { q: "Explain why using large amounts of chemical fertilisers and pesticides pollutes the environment.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Chemical fertilisers are leached into water bodies, causing eutrophication and algal blooms, and oxygen depletion kills fish.\nPesticides kill non-target organisms (beneficial insects, soil organisms), disrupting the ecological balance.\nPesticides (such as DDT) are hard to break down and are biomagnified along food chains.\nLong-term use makes pests develop resistance, requiring stronger pesticides.\nOrganic farming, biological control and crop rotation should be used as sustainable methods." },
      { q: "Write down the effects of land reclamation on the environment.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Land reclamation destroys marine habitats (mangroves, coral reefs, marine habitats), reducing biodiversity.\nIt increases suspended silt particles, clogging fish gills, reducing sunlight penetration, and affecting aquatic organisms.\nThe silt may contain toxic substances.\nIt changes the coastal ecology and water currents, affecting fisheries.\n(Land reclamation in Hong Kong has destroyed the habitats of Chinese white dolphins and corals.)" },
      { q: "Explain the mechanism of the greenhouse effect, and write down the main greenhouse gases.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Solar radiation reaches the Earth; the surface absorbs it and reflects heat (infrared radiation).\nGreenhouse gases (carbon dioxide, water vapour, methane, etc.) trap part of the infrared radiation, preventing it from being completely radiated into space.\nTherefore the atmospheric temperature rises (the greenhouse effect).\nThe natural greenhouse effect keeps the Earth's temperature suitable for life; human activities (burning fossil fuels) increase greenhouse gases, intensifying the greenhouse effect (global warming)." },
      { q: "Explain why deforestation intensifies global warming and soil erosion.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Forests absorb carbon dioxide by photosynthesis; after deforestation, the ability to absorb carbon dioxide decreases, so atmospheric carbon dioxide increases, intensifying global warming.\nBurning or rotting trees releases carbon dioxide.\nTree roots hold the soil; after deforestation the soil loses protection and is washed away by rain, causing soil erosion.\nHabitat destruction reduces biodiversity.\nForests should be protected and afforestation carried out (carbon sinks)." },
      { q: "Write down three air pollutants caused by human activities, with their sources and effects.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Carbon monoxide: from incomplete combustion (vehicles, factories); combines with haemoglobin, reducing the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood.\nSulphur dioxide: from burning sulphur-containing fuels; causes acid rain.\nNitrogen oxides: from vehicle and industrial emissions; cause acid rain and photochemical smog.\nSuspended particulates: from combustion and industrial emissions; damage the respiratory system.\n(Write three with sources and effects.)" },
      { q: "Explain why tropical rainforests are called the 'lungs of the Earth'.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The vegetation of tropical rainforests is dense and carries out a huge amount of photosynthesis.\nPhotosynthesis absorbs carbon dioxide and releases oxygen.\nThe rainforest absorbs a large amount of carbon dioxide, slowing global warming, and releases a large amount of oxygen.\nLike the gas-exchange function of the lungs, it is called the 'lungs of the Earth'.\nProtecting the rainforest is very important in mitigating climate change." },
      { q: "Write down the definition of sustainable development and explain its importance.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Sustainable development: meeting the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs.\nImportance: balances economic development with environmental protection, avoiding the exhaustion of resources.\nIt ensures the sustainable supply of resources, a healthy ecosystem, and the continued survival and development of future generations.\nMeasures include saving energy, using renewable energy, reducing pollution and conserving biodiversity." },
      { q: "Explain why using energy-saving appliances and public transport can reduce the carbon footprint.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Energy-saving appliances consume less electricity, reducing the burning of fossil fuels for power generation and reducing carbon dioxide emissions.\nPublic transport carries many passengers, so the per-person carbon emission is lower than that of private cars.\nReducing the use of private cars lowers fuel consumption and emissions.\nTherefore these measures reduce an individual's carbon footprint and mitigate global warming." }
    ]
  },
  {
    topicNo: 37,
    name: "Pollution Control and Conservation",
    questions: [
      { q: "Describe the processes of primary, secondary and tertiary treatment in sewage treatment.", marks: "6 marks", answer: "Primary treatment: screening, grit removal and settling, removing large solid waste and some suspended solids.\nSecondary treatment: in an aerobic environment, micro-organisms break down the organic matter in the sewage into inorganic compounds (ammonium compounds).\nThe ammonium compounds are converted into nitrates by the nitrification of nitrifying bacteria.\nIn an oxygen-deficient environment, nitrates are converted into nitrogen gas by the denitrification of denitrifying bacteria.\nTertiary treatment: physical and biological processes remove nutrients and remaining suspended solids, preventing algal blooms.\nBefore discharge, the water is usually disinfected to kill pathogens." },
      { q: "Compare 'in-situ conservation' and 'ex-situ conservation', giving one example of each.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "In-situ conservation is conserving organisms in their natural habitats, e.g. setting up country parks, marine parks and nature reserves.\nBenefit: organisms live in their natural habitats, preserving complete ecosystems.\nEx-situ conservation is conserving organisms outside their natural habitats, e.g. breeding endangered species in zoos, setting up seed banks.\nBenefit: can protect species whose numbers are extremely small and on the verge of extinction in the wild." },
      { q: "Write down the '4R' principles of solid waste, and explain the meaning of each.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Reduce: avoid the unnecessary consumption of materials and energy.\nReuse: use the same item repeatedly.\nRecycle: collect waste materials to make new products.\nReplace: use alternative resources to replace non-renewable or harmful resources.\nThe 4R principles reduce the amount of waste, save resources, and relieve the pressure on landfills." },
      { q: "Explain the process of sludge treatment and the use of its products.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The sludge produced in sewage treatment is broken down by anaerobic bacteria in an oxygen-deficient environment (digester).\nAnaerobic digestion produces methane gas.\nThe methane can be used as fuel (for power generation and heating).\nThe treated sludge can be used as fertiliser or disposed of properly.\nThis turns waste into useful resources and reduces pollution." },
      { q: "Write down the function of the catalytic converter in cars, and explain how it reduces air pollution.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "The catalytic converter is installed in the exhaust system of cars and converts harmful gases into less harmful gases.\nCarbon monoxide is oxidised into carbon dioxide.\nNitrogen oxides are reduced to nitrogen.\nUnburned hydrocarbons are oxidised into carbon dioxide and water.\nTherefore it reduces the air pollution caused by vehicle emissions." },
      { q: "Explain the principle of flue gas desulphurisation, and write down its benefits.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "In flue gas desulphurisation, lime (or lime slurry) is sprayed into the flue of a combustion facility (power plant).\nThe lime reacts with sulphur dioxide to form calcium sulphite/sulphate (gypsum).\nThe sulphur dioxide is removed and not released into the atmosphere.\nIt reduces sulphur dioxide emissions, alleviating the acid rain problem; the by-product gypsum can be used in construction." },
      { q: "Write down the aim of CITES (the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species) with examples.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "CITES is a convention regulating the international trade in endangered species.\nAim: to prevent endangered species from becoming extinct because of international trade.\nIt lists the trade restrictions or bans on endangered species (such as ivory, rhino horn).\nIt protects endangered species and maintains biodiversity." },
      { q: "Explain the benefits of preserving seeds in a seed bank.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "A seed bank preserves the genetic resources of a large number of plant seeds for a long time under low temperature and dry conditions.\nIt protects plant genetic diversity and prevents the extinction of species.\nSeeds can be taken out at any time for research, restoration and agricultural breeding.\nIt takes up little space and has a low preservation cost, and is an important method of ex-situ conservation.\n(For example, the Svalbard Global Seed Vault in Norway.)" },
      { q: "Write down the role of an Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA), and explain its importance.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "An EIA assesses the potential impacts of a project on the environment (ecology, water quality, air, noise, etc.) before it is carried out.\nIt identifies negative impacts and suggests mitigation measures.\nIt allows decision-makers to consider environmental factors, reducing the damage of the project to the environment.\nIt ensures sustainable development, balancing development and environmental protection.\n(Large projects in Hong Kong must undergo an EIA.)" },
      { q: "Explain the role of wetlands in pollution control and conservation.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Wetland plants and micro-organisms filter pollutants and absorb nutrients (nitrogen and phosphorus), purifying water quality.\nWetlands are the habitats and breeding grounds of many organisms, maintaining biodiversity.\nWetlands can regulate floods and protect the coast.\nThey are important ecosystems with conservation value.\n(Mai Po wetland is a habitat for migratory birds and is listed as a Ramsar wetland.)" },
      { q: "Compare the environmental effects of landfills and recycling.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Landfills: occupy land; produce leachate that pollutes groundwater and land; produce methane (a greenhouse gas) and odours; waste takes a long time to decompose.\nRecycling: turns waste materials into new products, reducing the pressure on landfills.\nRecycling saves resources (raw materials and energy) and reduces pollution.\nRecycling follows the 4R principles and is more environmentally friendly than landfilling.\nReduction, reuse and recycling should be prioritised, with landfilling only as the last resort." },
      { q: "Write down the individual and social measures to control water pollution.", marks: "5 marks", answer: "Individual: reduce the use of chemicals, dispose of waste properly (do not litter in the sea, do not rinse oil into drains), save water.\nSocial: sewage treatment plants treat sewage, regulate industrial wastewater discharge, prohibit direct discharge into the sea, monitor water quality.\nProtect water sources (catchwater areas), use natural fertilisers and reduce pesticides.\nEducate the public to protect water resources." },
      { q: "Explain why endangered species should be protected (four reasons).", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Economic: endangered species may have medicinal, food and other value; once lost they cannot be recovered.\nEcological: every species plays a role in the ecosystem; the loss of a species disrupts the ecological balance.\nAesthetic: biodiversity enriches the natural landscape for people to enjoy.\nMoral: humans have a responsibility to protect other species and avoid causing their extinction.\n(Add an explanation of the value of genetic diversity.)" },
      { q: "Write down three contributions that individuals can make to conservation.", marks: "3 marks", answer: "Reduce waste and support recycling (4R).\nSupport environmentally friendly products and green consumption.\nParticipate in conservation activities and support conservation organisations.\nDo not buy products made from endangered species, and do not damage country parks or natural habitats.\nSave resources (water and electricity) to reduce the burden on the environment." },
      { q: "Explain the measures of sustainable fisheries and their benefits.", marks: "4 marks", answer: "Measures: prohibit destructive fishing (trawling, fish bombing), set up closed seasons, limit the catch and net mesh size, aquaculture, artificial reef programmes.\nBenefits: give fish time to reproduce, allowing fish numbers to recover.\nProtect marine habitats and biodiversity.\nEnsure the sustainable supply of fishery resources, balancing the livelihoods of fishermen and ecological conservation." }
    ]
  }
];
